DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ccaammeerraattooppaamm  converts from any of dozens of raw camera image formats to
       PAM.

       Digital still cameras often can produce images in a special raw  format
       in  addition  to  something  more standard such as TIFF or JFIF (JPEG).
       Software supplied with the camera allows you to  manipulate  the  image
       using  information which is lost when the camera converts to the common
       format.  A particular camera model often has a unique raw format.




OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options  ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩  ), ccaammeerraattooppaamm recognizes
       the following command line options:



       --iiddeennttiiffyy__oonnllyy
              Report to Standard Error the format of the input image but don't
              generate  an output image.  Program fails if it cannot recognize
              the format.


       --vveerrbboossee
              Report to Standard Error details of the processing.


       --qquuiicckk__iinntteerrppoollaattee
              Use simple bilinear interpolation for quick  results.   The  de‐
              fault is to use a slow, high-quality adaptive algorithm.


       --hhaallff__ssiizzee
              Half-size  the  output  image.  Instead of interpolating, reduce
              each 2x2 block of  sensors  to  one  pixel.   Much  faster  than
              --qquuiicckk__iinntteerrppoollaattee.


       --ffoouurr__ccoolloorr__rrggbb
              Interpolate  RGB  as  four colors.  This causes a slight loss of
              detail, so use this only if you see false 2x2 mesh  patterns  in
              blue sky.


       --ddooccuummeenntt__mmooddee
              Show  the  raw  data as a grayscale image with no interpolation.
              This is good for photographing black and white documents.


       --bbaallaannccee__aauuttoo
              Automatic color balance.  The default is to use  a  fixed  color
              balance based on a white card photographed in sunlight.


       --bbaallaannccee__ccaammeerraa
              Use  the  color balance specified by the camera.  If ccaammeerraattooppaamm
              can't find this, it prints a warning and reverts to the default.


       --rreedd__ssccaallee==_f_l_o_a_t

       --bblluuee__ssccaallee_f_l_o_a_t
              Further adjust the color balance by multiplying the red and blue
              channels by these values.  Both default to 1.0.


       --bbrriigghhttnneessss==_f_l_o_a_t
              Change the output brightness.  Default is 1.0.


       --nnoo__cclliipp__ccoolloorr
              By default, ccaammeerraattooaappmm clips all colors to prevent pink hues in
              the highlights.  Combine this option  with  --bbrriigghhttnneessss==00..2255  to
              leave the image data completely unclipped.


       --rrggbb   Write raw camera colors to the output file.  By default, ccaammeerraa‐‐
              ttooaappmm converts to sRGB colorspace.


       --sseeccoonnddaarryy
              For cameras based on the Fuji Super CCD SR, this  option  causes
              ccaammeerraattooppaamm  to use the secondary sensors, in effect underexpos‐
              ing the image by four stops to reveal detail in the  highlights.
              ccaammeerraattooppaamm silently ignores this option for all other cameras.


       --lliinneeaarr
              This  option  causes  ccaammeerraattooppaamm to generate a variation on PAM
              that has "linear" color samples.  In true PAM,  each  sample  in
              the image raster is gamma-corrected; i.e. it is essentially pro‐
              portional to brightness.  With the  lliinneeaarr  option,  ccaammeerraattooppaamm
              generates an image in which the samples are instead proportional
              to light intensity.

              Without --lliinneeaarr, the image maxval is 255, so the image  contains
              one  byte per sample.  With --lliinneeaarr, the maxval is 65535, so the
              image contains two bytes per sample.

              Without --lliinneeaarr, ccaammeerraattooppaamm uses a 99th percentile white point.
              With --lliinneeaarr, it doesn't.  I don't know what that means.





